From d8fcd30a032f213ef2e5ea859ca7a81c0b696fc6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: IOS-POC-26-2b backport <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 02:33:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avformat/hls: anchor discontinuity sequences to the playlist
 timeline

Adapts FongMi/FFmpeg@5805f9364c2e9a5f6ce625c9077b308c3ed4014d for WebHTV
(IOS-POC-26-2b), on top of the n8.1.2 port of that commit.

5805f936 only corrects a timestamp jump larger than
max(2 * TARGETDURATION, 1 s) relative to the previous packet; smaller
backward jumps are clamped and smaller forward ones pass. Common ad layouts
jump by less: a pre-roll followed by content restarting near 0, a mid-roll
whose content clock skips the ad, back-to-back ads on their own clocks.
Linear playback then stayed 15-28 s off the playlist timeline, with
hundreds of pinned duplicate timestamps, and seeks disagreed with the shown
time.

For playlists that are finished when first normalized (EXT-X-ENDLIST):

- EXT-X-DISCONTINUITY is kept per segment. read_data_continuous() records
  the byte position at which each opened segment starts, and packets are
  attributed to their segment by pkt->pos, so late packets of the previous
  segment (demuxer and parser delay) are not taken for the new one.
- The first packet delivered from a new discontinuity sequence decides the
  offset of that sequence. It is re-anchored to its segment's playlist
  start (first_timestamp + the EXTINF durations before it + the stream
  baseline, the value seeking uses; RFC 8216 section 6.3.3, which is what
  AVPlayer and Media3's per-discontinuity TimestampAdjuster follow) only if,
  mapped with the current offset, it is more than 1 s away both from that
  start and from its own stream's continuity. Timestamps that really are
  continuous across the tag are therefore left exactly as stock delivers
  them, even with EXTINF drift (integer or rounded EXTINF). 1 s is about
  20 times the anchor error of a stream's first packet in a segment (up to
  ~53 ms) and well above per-segment EXTINF rounding, while real splices
  differ by seconds.
- The offset is shared by the playlist's streams, which keeps the
  segment's own A/V relation. Late packets of the previous sequence keep
  the previous offset. The first packet of each stream in a sequence is not
  clamped: a sub-frame splice overlap passes through as it does on a
  continuous splice. A re-anchor that would put the deciding stream a frame
  or more behind its last dts is moved to one frame after it instead of
  pinning a stretch of packets.
- After a seek, the anchor is taken from the seek stream's first packet
  instead of whichever packet comes first; the other streams' packets read
  before it are discarded anyway. The landing keyframe then maps exactly
  onto the seek threshold, and post-seek time-pos matches linear playback.

Live playlists keep the continuity-only mapping of 5805f936 unchanged.

The seek discard compared the packet dts rounded down to microseconds with
the threshold, while normalized timestamps are rounded to the nearest
tick, so a segment's first keyframe could compare 1 us short and be
dropped, landing one GOP late (EXTINF 15.166667, 16.633333, 6.666667 ...).
Compare in the packet's time base against the threshold rounded down
(ff_hls_timestamp_reached()). The caa3fa6a/e27ad576 clamp and baseline
semantics are unchanged.

No public API/ABI change: hls.c internals and the internal
hls_timestamp.{c,h} only. libavformat/tests/hls_timestamp.c gains cases for
within tolerance, EXTINF drift, pre-roll reset, mid-roll content skip,
backward and forward jumps, late packets, the one-frame absorb, the
seek-stream anchor and the rounding case.

[IOS-POC-26-2b: on the synthetic fixture matrix, linear |time-pos - EXTINF|
 <= 0.043 s on every VOD ad layout (port: up to 28.15 s), every in-range
 video seek lands, no dts is pinned more than once, M0/L4/DALL playback and
 seeks are byte-identical to stock, and LIVE/EVENT output is byte-identical
 to the port.]

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01H1rQEzEEyLNPgtsUgoq9T6
